3. Identify the bug and fix it:
class Counter {
    int count;
    public Counter(int count) {
        count = count;
    }
    public int getCount() { return count; }
}
Counter c = new Counter(10);
System.out.println(c.getCount()); // prints 0, not 10

items.a:
Bug: 'count = count' assigns the parameter to itself — the field this.count is never updated. Both sides resolve to the local parameter 'count', not the field.
Fix: use 'this.count = count;'
public Counter(int count) {
    this.count = count; // 'this.count' = field; 'count' = parameter
}
Now c.getCount() returns 10 correctly.

public class Circle {
    private double radius;

    // No-arg — default radius
    public Circle() {
        this(1.0);
    }

    // Parameterized — with validation
    public Circle(double radius) {
        if (radius <= 0)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Radius must be positive, got: " + radius);
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    // Copy constructor
    public Circle(Circle other) {
        this(other.radius); // Delegates to parameterized — reuses validation
    }

    public double getArea() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
    public double getRadius() { return radius; }

    @Override
    public String toString() { return "Circle(r=" + radius + ")"; }
}
// Usage:
Circle c1 = new Circle();       // radius=1.0
Circle c2 = new Circle(5.0);    // radius=5.0
Circle c3 = new Circle(c2);     // radius=5.0 — independent copy
System.out.println(c3.getArea()); // 78.53...

